"""Sequential refinements after exact primary-source checks; never infer verdicts from headings."""
by_num={int(c['id'].split('-')[-1])-45000:c for c in claims}
def update(n,**kwargs):
    by_num[n].update(kwargs)
    if 'fate' in kwargs:
        by_num[n]['present_status']='As of 19 November 2026: '+kwargs['fate']
update(26,status_on_pages='open on the pages',**{'class':'F'},statement='If N₁ means the normalizer of a maximal torus in an arbitrary semisimple G₁, its Weyl extension need not split. If it instead means the adjoint normalizer or a splitting is being assumed, the SL₂ counterexample does not decide that reading.',fate='SGA3 and Conrad §5.1 distinguish Weyl representatives from a group-theoretic splitting. The manuscript explicitly warns at c45:45 line294 to distinguish G₁ and its adjoint H₁; the overwritten normalizer claim is therefore retained as an unresolved scope question, with a counterexample only to the unrestricted interpretation.',confidence='medium')
update(105,refs=['AG45-MilneAG'],fate='Milne Algebraic Groups (2022), Theorem4.27 and Lemma4.28, prove that every closed subgroup of an affine algebraic group is the stabilizer of a line in a finite-dimensional representation. The latter lemma gives the exterior-power reduction explicitly.')
update(124,refs=['AG45-Zibrowius2018','AG45-EKM2008'],statement='Exterior powers give the special λ-ring structure on GW. For a connected semilocal commutative ring with2 invertible, its γ-filtration is the filtration by powers of the rank-zero augmentation ideal; the induced filtration onW is by powers ofI.',fate='Zibrowius Theorem2.5 establishes the λ-ring structure and Proposition4.1 states the exact semilocal filtration equality. On a general scheme the higher classical and γ-filtrations need not agree, so the conventions in the letter remain relevant.',fate_date='arXiv first12November2014; opened version11August2016, dated12August2016; online publication14December2017, journal issueJune2018.',confidence='high')
update(135,statement='The category of geometric points with specialization arrows is equivalent to the category of points of the étale topos. The latter is, by definition in SGA4VIII7.8, opposite to the category of fibre functors.',fate='SGA4 VIII7.8–7.9, original pp398–402 (Orgogozo re-edition PDF229–231), is the direct published counterpart. Both the variance convention and the pro-representability proof were checked; the remaining H48 pages are an editorial outline.')
update(177,**{'class':'B'},refs=['AG45-Bertolin2009','AG45-FGAIV'],fate='Bertolin Proposition1.3.1 (PDFp10) proves representability, separatedness, unramifiedness and essential properness over arbitraryS, using Artin representability, rigidity and the Néron property. FGA IV4.c supplies the author’s earlier projective representability framework. The phrase constant geometric fibres means that each geometric fibre is a constant group scheme; it does not assert constant Hom rank acrossS.',fate_date='Bertolin publishedMath.Z.261(2009),845–868, online2008; arXiv posting22July2010 is later than publication. FGAIV lectureMay1961.')
update(185,refs=['AG45-BCS2025','AG45-Robert2023'],fate='Bouthier–Česnavičius–Scavia §3.2 formula(3.2.1.1) gives full faithfulness for abelian-scheme torsors from a regular base to its generic points. The explicit local extension-and-descent argument here yields the claimedH¹ injection.')
update(186,**{'class':'B'},refs=['AG45-BCS2025'],statement='LetS be regular, Z⊂S closed ofcodimension≥2, andG/S an abelian scheme. Restriction is an equivalence betweenG-torsors onS and onS−Z. For a regular integral noetherian base this yields the stated codimension-one detection of a generic torsor’s extendability.',fate='Bouthier–Česnavičius–Scavia §4.2 formula(4.2.0.1) states and proves this exact abelian-torsor purity, using generic full faithfulness and finite-flat torsor purity. The separate completion-invariance note is CL-SA18-45204; no Vasiu–Zink abelian-model counterexample refutes this fixed-G assertion.',fate_date='Opened author PDF dated6June2025 records the established theorem; its finite-flat input cites Moret-Bailly1985 lemma2. No claim of first discovery or first circulation is made.',confidence='high')
update(203,computation='OverC choose a nonhyperelliptic curveC ofgenus≥3. If−1 onJ(C) were induced byf∈Aut(C), thenf acts as−1 onH⁰(K_C), so it acts trivially on the projectivized canonical embedding. Since that embedding is a closed immersion, f is the identity, whose Jacobian action is+1, contradiction. This directly checks the marked-sign counterexample without assuming the sought lift.')
cl(204,[],51,[23],K512,'302–306','The local obstruction group does not change under completion.','The margin proposes invariance under completion ofH¹(K,G)/H¹(R,G) for aDVRR and its abelian schemeG. The assertion needs a henselian/excellent or other exact local hypothesis; it is not part of torsor purity alone.','open on the pages','C','The opened purity theorem BCS§4.2 concerns deletion of codimension≥2 subsets, not completion of a nonhenselianDVR. Néron-model and henselian-valued cohomology results are relevant but do not certify the unrestricted line. The precise completion comparison remains a scoped literature question.',['AG45-BCS2025','AG45-Robert2023'],confidence='medium')
by_num[204]=claims[-1]
update(9,refs=['AG45-Abdulkareem2017','AG45-NR1966','AG45-NR1967'],fate='The bracket-defect calculation gives the relative lifting mechanism with the stated module hypotheses. Abdulkareem–Rakhimov–Said Husain §3, Theorem 1, restates the Nijenhuis–Richardson rigidity theorem for complex finite-dimensional Lie algebras. The original 1966/1967 full texts were not accessible; the characteristic-zero result alone is not used to certify every relative case.')
update(10,refs=['AG45-Abdulkareem2017','AG45-NR1967'],fate='Abdulkareem–Rakhimov–Said Husain §3, Theorem 1, gives the exact H²=0 rigidity implication over C and attributes it to Nijenhuis–Richardson. Formal rigidity also follows directly from the successive square-zero lifting calculation. No converse is asserted.')
update(11,refs=['AG45-NR1966','AG45-Abdulkareem2017'],fate='The saved Jacobi-defect calculation verifies the obstruction degree and the separate role of H². Later Lie-deformation literature uses this hierarchy; the original Nijenhuis–Richardson papers were traced bibliographically but their full text was inaccessible, so no original theorem number is supplied.')
update(57,fate='EKM Remark 35.31 states I³=0 for local fields and follows references back to Lam VI.2.15 and VI.2.24; Example 36.2(6) gives u=4. The rank-zero GW subgroup maps isomorphically to I, whose exponent divides four here. This does not say that the full Witt group has exponent four: W(Q₂) contains an element of order eight.')
update(63,refs=['AG45-StacksKummer','AG45-EKM2008','AG45-CDHH2026'],fate='The Kummer sequence and the Dedekind localization theory in Calmès et al., Hermitian K-theory III, §§2–3 (opened April 2026 revision), are exact adjacent results. Neither identifies the manuscript’s undefined F′(A) or its map to Pic(A)/2. This is an identification limit, not a declaration that the subject was undeveloped.')
