update(67,statement='For an integral prescheme, separatedness is equivalent to distinct points having no common dominating local subring in the common function field. In the separated case, x belongs to the closure of y iff O_x⊂O_y, and x↦O_x is injective.',fate='EGA I 8.2.2–8.2.4, printed p. 166, is an exact textual and mathematical match. The source is a reused typescript; its presence does not independently date the handwritten sheets.',fate_date='Published EGA I, 1960; date of the reused draft sheet not inferred.',confidence='high')
update(68,statement='For an integral separated scheme whose underlying topological space is Noetherian, the family of local subrings determines the topology and structure sheaf, with Γ(U,O)=∩_(x∈U)O_x. For a dominant morphism of integral separated schemes, O_(f(x)) is the unique target local ring dominated by O_x.',fate='EGA I 8.2.1, 8.2.5–8.2.7, printed pp. 165–167, gives the exact published statements. The published topology assertion uses a generating family, whereas the draft calls it a basis; CL-SA18-45205 isolates that stronger wording.',fate_date='Published EGA I, 1960; first draft date not established.',confidence='high')
update(69,**{'class':'A'},statement='If X is an irreducible prescheme and f:X→Y is a separated local immersion (respectively a local isomorphism), then f is an immersion (respectively an open immersion). Local immersion has the source-local meaning fixed in EGA I.',fate='EGA I Proposition 8.2.8, printed pp. 167–168, is exactly the displayed proposition and supplies the continuation of its proof. The apparent identification gap is resolved by the original publication; no additional finite-type assumption is inserted.',fate_date='EGA I published 1960.',confidence='high')
update(80,statement='For every module M, right contraction on the graded dual of Λ(M), defined by (u⌞t)(z)=u(t∧z), satisfies u⌞(s∧t)=(u⌞s)⌞t. Finite projectivity is not needed for this functional identity.',computation='exterior_checks.py: equation (31) checked on every basis triple through rank six over Z; the arbitrary-rank proof is associativity of wedge followed by evaluation. See outputs/exterior_checks.json.')
update(81,statement='For a linear map f:M→N, transpose exterior pullback satisfies f*(u′⌞Λ(f)(t))=f*(u′)⌞t. For an isomorphism, the equivalent transported identity is equation (33).',computation='exterior_checks.py: 84 universal polynomial comparisons for equation (32) with a symbolic 3×3 matrix. The general proof moves Λ(f) through evaluation and uses multiplicativity; (33) follows by inversion.')
update(82,statement='For u=x₁*∧…∧x_q* and t∈Λ^p M with p≤q, u⌞t is the signed sum over p-element subsets H of {1,…,q} of ⟨t,x_H*⟩ x_K*, where K is the complementary subset.',computation='exterior_checks.py records the integral shuffle-sign proof. This is the right contraction of a q-covector by a p-vector, not the opposite variance of equation (36).')
update(84,computation='exterior_checks.py verifies the coefficients with sign ε_(K,H); the coproduct definition (1⊗evaluation)(Δ⊗1) gives the formula for arbitrary modules. The complement in the manuscript must be taken in {1,…,p}, not {1,…,q}.')
update(85,computation='exterior_checks.py: every basis triple through rank six satisfies equation (37). The general coefficient of a disjoint three-part partition is the sign of the same concatenation on both sides, proving the identity in every rank over Z and hence after base change.')
update(86,computation='exterior_checks.py: all 70 minors of every exterior degree for a universal 4×4 matrix agree exactly with the wedge coefficients. The arbitrary-rank proof is the alternating multilinear determinant expansion.')
update(169,refs=['AG45-KS1959','AG45-Robert2023'],fate='Koizumi–Shimura Theorem 4, printed p. 200, proves good reduction for a quotient of an abelian variety with good reduction; the isogeny case is explicit in the proof. Robert Theorem 3.1.10 gives the modern criterion. This does not assert that a chosen subgroup closure is smooth.',fate_date='Koizumi–Shimura 1959, received 3 October 1959; modern restatement in Robert draft 7 March 2023.')
update(171,refs=['AG45-KS1959','AG45-MilneAV','AG45-Robert2023'],fate='Koizumi–Shimura Theorem 4 (p. 200, proof pp. 204–205) gives the quotient statement. Poincaré complete reducibility, Milne Proposition 12.1, reduces the abelian-subvariety statement to it. The warning on p. 205 concerns a particular embedded model, a different assertion.')
update(172,refs=['AG45-KS1959','AG45-SGA3VIIA'],fate='Koizumi–Shimura’s explicit example on printed p. 205 has a subgroup specializing to p times a diagonal in a product of elliptic curves. It verifies the distinction between existence of an abstract good model and smoothness of the chosen closure. The separate equal-characteristic repair is CL-SA18-45173.',confidence='high')
update(176,refs=['AG45-EGAIV4','AG45-StacksUnramified','AG45-Bertolin2009'],fate='EGA IV 18.10.1 and Corollary 18.10.3, printed pp. 157–159, give the unramified-to-étale criterion over an integral geometrically unibranch base. The proper universally injective unramified criterion is Stacks Lemma 41.7.2. These precise hypotheses, not merely reducedness, are retained.')
by_num[204]['refs']=['AG45-Cesnavicius2020','AG45-BCS2025']
by_num[204]['fate']='Česnavičius, Invariance under Henselian pairs (2020), Theorems 1.4 and 2.1, gives precise invariance results. Its smooth quasi-affine hypothesis excludes abelian schemes, and its finite-flat invariance is in degrees at least two. Thus it does not establish the unrestricted quotient assertion. BCS §4.2 concerns codimension-two deletion, a different comparison.'
by_num[204]['present_status']='As of 19 November 2026: '+by_num[204]['fate']
cl(205,[60],45,[39],P,'163–171','The rational-function domains D(f) form a basis.','The text proves that the domains D(f) generate the topology: finite intersections give a basis. The stronger assertion that individual D(f) already form a basis is not supplied by this proof.','proof sketched','C','EGA I Corollary 8.2.5 and the following paragraph explicitly use a generating family and finite intersections. This is a precise difference between draft and publication; no counterexample to the stronger statement under every possible intended hypothesis is asserted.',['AG45-EGAI'],confidence='high',date='Published corrected wording EGA I, 1960.')
update(70,fate='Godement, Chapter II, Lemma 3.8.1 (printed p. 158), gives the exact locally finite refinement condition used here. The PFII shorthand remains an unidentified internal reference; its definition is not invented.')
update(71,fate='Godement, Chapter II, §3.7, printed pp. 157–158, constructs smooth separating functions by convolution and proves that the smooth-function sheaf is fine, hence soft.')
update(72,fate='Godement, Chapter II, Theorem 3.7.1 (printed p. 156), proves that every module over a soft unital ring sheaf on the stated paracompactifying support family is soft. A quotient ring is such a module; no assertion about arbitrary quotients of soft abelian sheaves is needed.')
update(73,fate='Godement, Chapter II, §3.6 (printed p. 155), defines the locally finite sum sectionwise and proves its partition statement. Local finiteness is the essential hypothesis.')
update(74,fate='Godement, Chapter II, Theorem 3.6.1 (printed pp. 155–156), gives a partition of any section subordinate to an open cover. Applying it to the unit of the ring sheaf gives the manuscript construction.')
update(113,fate='SGA3 VIIA 3.1.1–3.1.2 gives dual coalgebras and group-like points; VIIB 2.2.1, printed pp. 310–311, gives the equivalence for topologically flat formal groups and flat bialgebras. The earlier locator 2.6 concerned Lie enveloping algebras and is corrected here.')
update(120,refs=['AG45-EKM2008','AG45-Zibrowius2018'],statement='For a field k of characteristic different from two, let J be the rank-zero ideal of GW(k) and I the fundamental ideal of W(k). The quotient map identifies Jⁿ with Iⁿ for n>0. The positive associated graded is Milnor K-theory modulo two, with its quadratic Steinberg presentation; degree zero of gr_J GW is Z. This is not a formal consequence of an arbitrary inhomogeneous presentation.',fate='Zibrowius Proposition 4.1 and Remark 4.2 identify the two positive filtrations. The Milnor-conjecture results stated in EKM §42 establish the positive graded comparison. Ordinary Stiefel–Whitney classes have different indexing: EKM Lemma 5.6 and Corollary 5.9 give first possible index 2^(n−1) on the nth augmentation power, so one must not replace the Milnor invariant by w_n in all degrees.')
update(121,refs=['AG45-EKM2008','AG45-Zibrowius2018'],fate='The identification requires both EKM Proposition 5.3, giving e₂:I²/I³≅K₂^M(k)/2, and EKM Theorem 44.1, the degree-two norm-residue isomorphism onto Br(k)[2]. Zibrowius Remark 4.2 transfers this to the rank-zero Grothendieck–Witt filtration. Together they settle the precise field injectivity question; Merkurjev alone without the first identification would leave a logical gap.',fate_date='Milnor degree-two graded comparison 1970; Merkurjev norm-residue theorem 1981; opened exact formulations in EKM 2008. Manuscript letter dated 22 July 1961.')
cl(206,[],51,[15],K512,'69–73','Can a proper group scheme in characteristic zero have a greatest abelian subscheme? What if the base is reduced?','Over a characteristic-zero field, a proper group scheme of finite type has a smooth connected identity component, which is an abelian variety. Over a reduced characteristic-zero base, a greatest abelian subscheme requires a relative flatness and dimension statement not provided by the field argument.','open on the pages','C','SGA3 VIB gives relative identity components under explicit smoothness or flatness hypotheses. The word proper alone in the proposed question does not supply them. The question remains a formulation-and-hypothesis problem in this dossier, not an assertion that all relevant relative structure theory is unknown.',['AG45-SGA3VIB','AG45-Conrad2014'],confidence='medium')
